<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samarkand</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samarkand is an ancient city in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and one of the major centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, fell within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and his successors adorned it with monumental architecture. It also features in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> profile and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> profile, which notes that Timur carried artisans there after sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1398.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarkand.jpg" alt="Samarkand" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Registan_-_Gusjer.jpg: Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain derivative work: MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
